---
title: Refinement Lifecycle
config:
    layout: elk
---
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB

    [*] --> raw: issue created
    [*] --> ready_bounced: from process inner-loop

    raw --> refining: PM claims raw

    refining --> duplicate: PM marks duplicate
    refining --> wont_fix: PM marks won't-fix

    refining --> consult_requested: PM requests consult
    consult_requested --> consulting: consultant claims consult

    consulting --> consult_complete: consultant posts findings
    consult_complete --> refining: PM claims consult feedback

    consulting --> spiking: consultant creates spike sub-issue
    spiking --> consulting: consultant claims spike findings

    refining --> ready_for_dev: PM marks ready (feat/bug/chore)
    refining --> ready_for_experiment: PM marks ready (exp)

    ready_for_dev --> [*]: to process inner-loop
    ready_for_experiment --> [*]: to process inner-loop

    ready_bounced --> refining: PM claims bounced issue

    refining --> deprioritized: PM parks issue
    ready_for_dev --> deprioritized: PM parks issue
    ready_for_experiment --> deprioritized: PM parks issue

    deprioritized --> refining: PM claims to re-refine (staleness check)
    deprioritized --> wont_fix: PM kills parked issue

    duplicate --> [*]: terminal (deduplicated)
    wont_fix --> [*]: terminal (abandoned)

    note right of raw
        Entry: issue created by anyone.
        Exits: ready_for_dev, ready_for_experiment, deprioritized, duplicate, wont_fix.

        Cross-process legend:
        • Entry shared states: ready_bounced (from inner-loop).
        • Exit shared states: ready_for_dev, ready_for_experiment (to inner-loop).
        • Spawned work items (external, not shared): spike sub-issue is created during consulting and starts as ready_for_spike on the inner-loop diagram.
    end note

    note right of consulting
        Consultant is the working owner. They may either post findings directly or commission a spike sub-issue to gather more info. Spike is a separate issue that enters the inner loop as ready_for_spike.
    end note

    note right of spiking
        Parent issue is resting while the spike is worked. Creating a spike has the side effect of spawning a new work item on the inner-loop diagram (ready_for_spike); that spawned item is not a shared-state handoff because it is a new issue, not the parent.

        The consultant polls for their spike's completion and re-claims the parent when findings are posted. The claim is a normal claim transition — the external trigger (spike completes) doesn't bypass the claim-before-work rule.
    end note

    note right of ready_bounced
        Shared resting state with inner-loop. A developer in inner-loop.implementing decided the issue wasn't actually ready and bounced it back. PM claims to re-refine.
        This is the "iterated" terminal path from inner-loop's perspective — but the work item is not terminal, it re-enters refinement.
    end note

    note right of ready_for_dev
        Shared resting state with inner-loop. Queue for inner-loop developer polling. Claim SLA: hours.
        Owned by inner-loop once a developer claims.

        Prioritization is continuous and attribute-based: PM maintains queue order via priority labels and rank. Re-ranking does not change state; pulling from the top is the existing transition to inner-loop.
    end note

    note right of ready_for_experiment
        Shared resting state with inner-loop. Same as ready_for_dev but feeds the experimentation process after release. Claim SLA: hours.
    end note

    note right of deprioritized
        Resting. Issue was refined (or mid-refinement) and PM has decided it should not be worked on now — priorities shifted, the underlying need receded, or a better approach emerged. Semi-terminal: the item is out of the pullable queue but not closed.

        Re-entry always routes through refining, not directly back to ready. The PM claims, runs a staleness check (is the problem still real? is the AC still right? have dependencies changed?), and either flips back to ready quickly (if still valid) or re-refines properly (if stale). This guards against the failure mode where a parked item returns with outdated assumptions and a developer picks it up.

        Kill from parked goes to wont_fix (single terminal — same semantic as killed during refining).
    end note

    note right of wont_fix
        Terminal (abandoned). Covers all "we're not doing this" outcomes regardless of origin state — killed during refining, or killed from deprioritized. Always includes a reason and a reopen condition.
    end note
